---
interface Props {
  class?: string;
}

const { class: className = "legal-article" } = Astro.props;
---
<article class={className}>
  <p>
    <strong>Jason Holt Digital LLC</strong> is the data controller for both Mythic GME Mobile and Mythic GME Digital. I'm
    the only person who works on the apps and the only person who reads privacy requests. For any privacy question, email
    <a href="mailto:privacy@jasonholtdigital.com">privacy@jasonholtdigital.com</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Short Version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The apps have no accounts and never ask for your name, email address or phone number.</li>
    <li>Your Journals, Scenes, Lists, tables, notes, questions and rolls stay on your device unless you choose to export, share, sync or send them.</li>
    <li>I don't sell data, and nothing goes to advertisers or data brokers. Nothing is used to track you across other companies' apps or websites.</li>
    <li>I don't use any advertising or analytics product from Google, Microsoft or Amazon. Some of my service providers rent servers from them (see <a href="#google-microsoft-amazon">Google, Microsoft and Amazon</a>).</li>
    <li>From version 1.7, error reports are on so I can fix crashes, and you can turn them off. Basic counts and Usage sharing stay off until you say yes. You can change each answer at any time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Apps This Covers</h2>
  <p>This privacy policy applies to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mythic GME Mobile</strong> — iPhone, iPad, Android, and Fire tablets</li>
    <li><strong>Mythic GME Digital</strong> — Windows, macOS, and Linux, including the versions sold on itch.io</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Version 1.7 changes what the apps send. The next sections describe 1.7 and later.
    <a href="#before-1-7">Versions before 1.7</a> describes the version most people have installed today.
  </p>

  <h2>What Stays on Your Device</h2>
  <p>
    The Mythic GME apps are local-first. Your Journals, Scenes, Threads, Characters, Lists, custom tables, notes, Fate
    Questions, dice rolls and table results are stored on your device, or in iCloud if you turn on iCloud sync. I don't
    host your game content on any server of mine.
  </p>

  <h2>What the App Sends (Version 1.7 and Later)</h2>

  <h3>Purchases</h3>
  <p>
    When you buy or restore Expanded Features from the App Store, Google Play or the Amazon Appstore, RevenueCat checks the
    store receipt so the app can unlock what you bought. RevenueCat receives the receipt, transaction ID, product, price and
    store, and gives your installation a random ID of its own. On iPhone and iPad, its code also sends the identifier Apple
    gives each app developer for your device, which can't be used across other developers' apps. The versions sold on
    itch.io and other direct downloads don't use RevenueCat.
  </p>

  <h3>Updates</h3>
  <p>
    When the app starts, and every so often while it's open, it asks Shorebird whether a fix is available for your version and downloads it if there is one.
    The request includes the app's ID and version, your platform and processor type, the fix number you already have, and a
    random ID that Shorebird's updater creates for your installation. Shorebird uses that ID only to count installations.
  </p>

  <h3>Basic Counts (Optional)</h3>
  <p>
    With basic counts on, the app sends PostHog a count once a day when you use it, one when a session starts, one the
    first time it counts, and, once each, one when you come back 1, 7 or 30 days after first opening the app. A count
    carries the app version and build, the store you installed from, your platform and operating-system version, your
    layout, and whether Expanded Features is unlocked. PostHog works out your country from your network address and
    doesn't keep the address.
  </p>
  <p>
    Counts carry a random ID that the app replaces at the start of each calendar month, so counts from one month can't
    be connected to another. I use the counts to see how many people use the app and whether they come back. They stay
    off until you turn them on, on the first-run screen or in Settings, under Analytics.
  </p>

  <h3>Error Reports</h3>
  <p>
    When something goes wrong, the app sends an error report so I can find and fix the bug. A report contains the error
    type, where it happened in the app's code, a short description I wrote for that error, the kinds of steps the app
    took just before (such as opening a dialog), the app version and update number, and your device model and operating
    system. With error reports on, the app also sends how long it takes to start and to open screens, and how smoothly
    screens draw, for a sample of sessions, and whether a session ended in a crash, so I can see which versions are stable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reports carry a random ID for your installation, so I can
    tell one installation hitting a bug many times from many installations hitting it once. That ID is never connected
    to your basic counts, Usage sharing, purchases or anything that identifies you. Sentry works out your country from
    your network address and doesn't keep the address. Reports never include your Journal names, file names, folder
    paths or anything you wrote.
  </p>
  <p>
    Error reports go to Sentry and PostHog. They're on from the start, and you can turn them off on the first-run screen
    or in Settings, under Analytics.
  </p>

  <h3>Usage Sharing (Optional)</h3>
  <p>
    With Usage sharing on, I also receive which features you use and the kind of action you take, such as asking a Fate
    Question or rolling dice, so I can see which tools help and where people get stuck. I never receive what you write or
    the results you roll. These records also carry the details sent with basic counts, including your country, and the
    app's language. They share one random usage ID, and records from the same session share a session ID, so I can see
    how features are used over time. PostHog keeps a profile for the usage ID with your latest app version, platform,
    layout and app language, and whether Expanded Features is unlocked. The usage ID is created when you turn Usage sharing on, is never connected to your purchases or error reports, and is thrown away when
    you turn Usage sharing off.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you turn Usage sharing off, the app stops sending usage records, deletes any it hasn't sent yet, and asks me to
    delete the ones it sent. I delete them within 30 days. You can see your usage ID in Settings, under Analytics, while
    Usage sharing is on.
  </p>

  <h3>Feature Switches</h3>
  <p>
    When the app starts and when you return to it, it asks PostHog which features to turn on or off, for example to
    switch off a feature that's causing problems. Each request uses a new random ID and carries the app version and
    build, your platform, the store you installed from, and a random number from 0 to 99 that the app stores once, so a
    change can reach a share of installations first. The request doesn't include your location. This happens whatever
    your answers above, because a fix has to reach every installation.
  </p>

  <h3>Feature Requests</h3>
  <p>
    When you open Feature Requests, the voting board at Features.vote loads inside the app. It receives an ID the app
    stores for your installation, made from your platform, your operating-system version and the time it was created, so
    your votes count once. The board shows you as anonymous unless you choose to sign in. Nothing is sent to Features.vote
    unless you open Feature Requests.
  </p>

  <h3>What the App Never Sends</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Journals, Scenes, Threads, Characters, Lists, custom tables, notes, or anything you write</li>
    <li>Fate Questions, dice values, or results</li>
    <li>Recordings of your screen</li>
    <li>Your name, email address, or phone number</li>
    <li>Your city, address or exact location</li>
    <li>Your advertising ID</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="before-1-7">Versions Before 1.7</h2>
  <p>This is what versions 1.6 and earlier send. Most people have 1.6.7 installed today.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Error reports</strong> go to Sentry from every installed copy. They include the error, the app version, your
      device model and operating system, a sample of performance timings, and counts of app sessions. To help me fix
      problems with how Journals are named, opened and saved, a report about a file error can include the name of the
      Journal or file and the folder it was in, which may contain your computer's user name. Version 1.7 no longer sends
      these details.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Analytics</strong> is off unless you turned it on. With it on, iPhone, iPad, Android and Mac send PostHog one
      record a day with the app version, store, platform, operating-system version, your language setting, and whether
      Expanded Features is unlocked, together with a random ID PostHog's code keeps for your installation. PostHog works
      out your country from your network address. With analytics on,
      error reports to Sentry also carried a random ID the app created for your device. Windows and Linux don't send
      analytics.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Session Replay</strong> is off unless an adult turned it on. With it on, iPhone, iPad and Android send Sentry a
      recording when an error happens, with text and images masked. Version 1.7 removes Session Replay.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Purchases</strong> and <strong>updates</strong> work as described for 1.7.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who Receives Data</h2>
  <p>
    These companies receive data from the apps. PostHog, Sentry and RevenueCat process it on my behalf under data
    processing agreements, and only for the purposes in this policy. Shorebird handles its update data under its own
    privacy policy.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>PostHog</strong> (basic counts, error reports, Usage sharing and feature switches; analytics from
      versions before 1.7).
      Stored in the United States on Amazon Web Services. Transfers rely on the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and Standard
      Contractual Clauses. Retention depends on my plan; PostHog currently lists one year on its free plan and seven years
      on paid plans.
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://posthog.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy Policy</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://posthog.com/subprocessors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Subprocessors</a></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sentry</strong> (error reports, app start and screen timings, and crash-free rates, from every platform).
      Stored in the United States; Sentry lists Google Cloud and Amazon Web Services as its hosting providers. Transfers rely on the EU-U.S. Data Privacy
      Framework and Standard Contractual Clauses. Error data is kept for 30 to 90 days depending on its type.
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://sentry.io/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy Policy</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://sentry.io/legal/subprocessors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Subprocessors</a></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>RevenueCat</strong> (purchases, store builds only). Stored in the United States on Amazon Web Services, with
      Google Cloud among its listed providers. Transfers rely on Standard Contractual Clauses. Purchase records are kept as
      long as needed to restore what you bought.
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.revenuecat.com/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy Policy</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.revenuecat.com/dpa" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Data Processing Agreement</a></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shorebird</strong> (updates). Runs on Google Cloud in Iowa, United States, and delivers fixes from Google Cloud
      Storage. Shorebird keeps installation counts as anonymous tallies rather than a list of IDs.
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://shorebird.dev/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy Policy</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://docs.shorebird.dev/system/security/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Security</a></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Features.vote</strong> (Feature Requests, only when you open it). It handles the board and your votes under
      its own privacy policy.
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://features.vote/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy Policy</a></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="google-microsoft-amazon">Google, Microsoft and Amazon</h3>
  <p>
    I don't send your data to Google, Microsoft or Amazon for their own use, and I don't use their advertising or analytics
    products. Some of my providers store their data on servers rented from Amazon Web Services or Google Cloud, as listed
    above. Those companies host the data for my providers; they don't receive it to use for themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you get the app from the App Store, Google Play, the Amazon Appstore, the Microsoft Store or itch.io, that store
    handles your purchase and download under its own privacy policy. Your device's operating system may also send crash
    and diagnostic information to Apple, Google or Microsoft, depending on your device settings. Those stores and system
    makers decide how they use that information, not me.
  </p>

  <h2>I Don't Sell Your Data</h2>
  <p>
    I don't sell or share personal information for advertising, I don't use targeted advertising, and I don't work with
    data brokers. If you want to send a "Do Not Sell or Share" request anyway, email
    <a href="mailto:privacy@jasonholtdigital.com">privacy@jasonholtdigital.com</a> and I'll honor any applicable right.
  </p>

  <h2>Your Choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Basic counts:</strong> Settings, Analytics. Off until you turn them on.</li>
    <li><strong>Error reports:</strong> Settings, Analytics. On until you turn them off.</li>
    <li><strong>Usage sharing:</strong> Settings, Analytics. Off until you turn it on.</li>
    <li><strong>First-run dialog:</strong> the first time you open 1.7, the app explains these choices. Error reports start on; nothing else is sent until you turn it on.</li>
    <li><strong>Your device:</strong> your operating system's privacy settings control what it shares with Apple, Google or Microsoft.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Legal Basis (GDPR)</h2>
  <p>For people in the European Union and the United Kingdom, I rely on:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Providing the service you asked for:</strong> checking purchases and delivering updates.</li>
    <li><strong>Consent:</strong> basic counts and Usage sharing in version 1.7 and later, and analytics in earlier versions. You can withdraw it at any time in Settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Legitimate interests:</strong> error reports, so I can keep the app working, and feature switches, so a fix can reach every installation. In version 1.7 and later you can object to error reports at any time by turning them off in Settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Replying to you:</strong> when you email me.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your Privacy Rights</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, privacy laws may give you rights to access, correct, delete, export, restrict, or object
    to processing of your personal information, to withdraw consent, and to complain to a privacy regulator.
  </p>
  <p>
    In version 1.7 and later, basic counts and error reports can't be matched to you, by design: counts carry a random ID
    that changes every month, and error reports carry one the app doesn't show you, so I can't find them for one
    person. Two things can be matched: Usage sharing records, by your usage
    ID, which you can copy from Settings, under Analytics; and purchase records, by your store transaction ID.
  </p>
  <p>
    Versions before 1.7 used IDs the app doesn't show you: PostHog's ID for analytics, and a device ID in some error
    reports. I can't see those IDs or connect them to you, so I can't find or delete one person's records from those
    versions. Turning analytics off in those versions stops new analytics records, and error reports expire from Sentry
    within 90 days.
  </p>
  <p>
    For purchases, I can look up and delete your RevenueCat record if you send me your store transaction ID or receipt.
  </p>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:privacy@jasonholtdigital.com">privacy@jasonholtdigital.com</a> with your request. I'll respond
    within the time the law requires. Please don't send Journal content to identify yourself; I don't need it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Your game data</strong> stays on your device or in the sync service you turn on. Export anything you want to
    keep before uninstalling.
  </p>

  <h2>Children's Privacy</h2>
  <p>The apps are not directed to children under 13. They don't require accounts or ask for a birth date, so I generally don't know a player's age.</p>
  <p>
    On devices that report the player's age range (Google Play on Android, and Apple's age range on iPhone, iPad and Mac),
    the app uses that signal on the device only. It is never stored and never sent. When the signal shows the player is
    under 18, the app hides and turns off basic counts and Usage sharing, and doesn't create a usage ID. Error reports
    stay on so the app keeps working, with the same switch to turn them off, and without country, timings or crash-free
    rates.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a player under 13, the error report ID is used only to keep the app working, never to contact the player, build
    a profile or show ads. Sentry keeps error data for 30 to 90 days.
  </p>
  <p>If you believe a child has sent personal information through the apps or a support email, write to <a href="mailto:privacy@jasonholtdigital.com">privacy@jasonholtdigital.com</a> so I can review and delete it.</p>

  <h2>App Store Privacy Labels</h2>
  <h3>Apple</h3>
  <p>
    My App Store label lists the data above as <strong>Data Not Linked to You</strong>: none of it is connected to your
    name, email or an account, and I never try to connect it. Nothing is listed as used to track you.
  </p>
  <h3>Google Play</h3>
  <p>
    My Google Play Data safety form lists app activity, crash logs and diagnostics, approximate location, device or other
    IDs, and purchase history as collected, encrypted in transit, and not shared.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to This Policy</h2>
  <p>
    When I change this policy, I'll update the date at the top. If a change affects what the app sends, I'll say so in
    the app's release notes, and the app will ask again before turning on anything that needs your yes.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact Me</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:privacy@jasonholtdigital.com">privacy@jasonholtdigital.com</a>, or write to Jason Holt Digital
    LLC, 1111b S Governors Avenue STE 21904, Dover, DE 19904, USA.
  </p>
</article>
